            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Pompeius</strong><br>\nGnaeus Pompeius Magnus, known as Pompey the Great, was born in 106 BC to an aristocratic Roman family. His father was a successful general, which allowed Pompey to grow up amidst the turmoil of Roman politics and war. Pompey proved his military talent early on by winning a battle in Africa and celebrated a triumph in Rome, an honor usually reserved for seasoned generals, by the age of 25.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nPompey's military skill and the influence of his family helped him rise quickly in the Roman hierarchy. His victories against the Marian forces in Sicily and Africa granted him the recognition of Sulla, who was the ruler of Rome at the time. These successes earned him the title \"Magnus\" or \"the Great\" and helped to set the stage for his illustrious career as one of Rome's greatest commanders.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Campaign in the East</strong><br>\nIn 67 BC, Pompey was given extraordinary powers to clear the Mediterranean Sea of pirates, which he accomplished within three months. Following this, he led Roman troops against Mithridates VI of Pontus, a formidable enemy of Rome. His campaign in the East extended Roman dominions and laid the groundwork for the Roman province of Asia.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
