            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>First Triumvirate</strong><br>\nPompey joined forces with Julius Caesar and Marcus Licinius Crassus in 60 BC to form the First Triumvirate, a political alliance that dominated Roman public life. The union was advantageous for Pompey as it secured land for his troops and furthered his political ambitions. However, the partnership was strained by rivalries and the death of Crassus in 53 BC.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Conflict with Julius Caesar</strong><br>\nAfter the First Triumvirate collapsed, Pompey's relationship with Caesar deteriorated. In 49 BC, Caesar crossed the Rubicon, leading to a civil war between the two formidable leaders. Pompey was considered the legitimate leader of Rome by the Senate, but he struggled to maintain his standing against Caesar's growing popularity and military success.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Battle of Pharsalus</strong><br>\nThe decisive encounter between Pompey and Caesar occurred at the Battle of Pharsalus in 48 BC. Despite having a larger army, Pompey's forces were defeated by Caesar's tactical genius. The defeat was crushing for Pompey, marking the downfall of his military and political career and altering the course of Roman history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
