          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Flight and Death</strong><br>\nAfter his defeat at Pharsalus, Pompey fled to Egypt, hoping to find refuge with the young Pharaoh Ptolemy XIII. However, in a bid to win Caesar's favor, Ptolemy's advisers betrayed Pompey, and he was assassinated as he stepped ashore on September 28, 48 BC. His death was a tragic end for a man who had once been hailed as one of Rome's greatest commanders.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nPompey's career left a significant mark on Rome. His military campaigns expanded Rome's territories and his political maneuvers contributed to the shaping of the Roman Empire. Despite his ultimate defeat and death, Pompey is remembered for his early accomplishments and his role in the transformative period of the late Roman Republic.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pompey in Historical Memory</strong><br>\nPompey's life and career are a testament to the volatility of Roman politics and the peril of personal ambition. He began his life in the shadows of the Sullan regime, reached the heights of power in an unstable alliance, and died amidst the conflict that gave rise to one of Rome's most famous dictators, Caesar. Pompey remains a figure studied for both his triumphs and his failures in the complex narrative of Roman history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
